    id: "0e684f1e7ff11d40eca7",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "China to launch Shenzhou-24 spaceship with first foreign astronaut",
    summary: "Pakistani national Muhammad Zeeshan Ali will be the first foreign astronaut to board China's space station.",
    preview: "Pakistani national Muhammad Zeeshan Ali will be the first foreign astronaut to board China's space station. JIUQUAN, Gansu: China will launch its Shenzhou-24 spaceship on Wednesday (Nov 25) at 8.18am local time (0018 GMT) with a crew including a Pakistani national, who will be the first foreign astronaut to board China's space station.",
    body: "Pakistani national Muhammad Zeeshan Ali will be the first foreign astronaut to board China's space station.\n\nJIUQUAN, Gansu: China will launch its Shenzhou-24 spaceship on Wednesday (Nov 25) at 8.18am local time (0018 GMT) with a crew including a Pakistani national, who will be the first foreign astronaut to board China's space station.\n\nThe three-person flight crew consists of commander Tang Hongbo, flight engineer Wu Yi and Pakistani payload specialist Muhammad Zeeshan Ali, whose participation is part of bilateral cooperation under Beijing's Belt and Road Initiative.\n\nThe crew is in good condition and launch preparations are complete, the China Manned Space Agency (CMSA) announced on Tuesday.\n\nChina's Shenzhou missions have sent trios of Chinese astronauts to the Tiangong station for six-month stays since 2021.\n\nAli is scheduled for a short-term visit before returning with two of the outgoing Shenzhou-23 crew.\n\nZhu Yangzhu from the Shenzhou-23 crew will be staying in space for a year-long mission, authorities said.\n\nThe launch comes amid an accelerating race to the moon with the United States, which alleges that Beijing plans to colonise and mine lunar territory and resources - claims Beijing has strongly rejected.\n\nShenzhou-24, launching aboard a Long March-2F rocket from the Jiuquan Satellite Launch Center, is the 41st flight mission of China's crewed space programme and will complete an in-orbit rotation with the Shenzhou-23 crew, authorities said.\n\nIn orbit, the crew will carry out space science experiments alongside planned station research.",
