    id: "640fe156876e0b026e50",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "EU plans sports ban in sweeping new Russia sanctions proposal",
    summary: "Proposed sanctions aim to curb Russian crypto financing and 'propaganda' in sports.",
    preview: "Proposed sanctions aim to curb Russian crypto financing and ‘propaganda’ in sports. The European Commission has proposed new sanctions against Russia targeting its banks, crypto platforms, and participation in sporting events. Commission chief Ursula von der Leyen introduced the 22nd package of sanctions on Monday, which includes a ban on Russian teams and individual athletes competing under the country’s flag at events in the European Union.",
    body: "Proposed sanctions aim to curb Russian crypto financing and ‘propaganda’ in sports.\n\nThe European Commission has proposed new sanctions against Russia targeting its banks, crypto platforms, and participation in sporting events.\n\nCommission chief Ursula von der Leyen introduced the 22nd package of sanctions on Monday, which includes a ban on Russian teams and individual athletes competing under the country’s flag at events in the European Union.\n\n“We are striking harder at Russia’s ways to finance its war, by targeting its banks and crypto platforms,” von der Leyen wrote in a post on X, adding that the EU will be “shutting the door to its participation in European sports and cultural events”.\n\nEconomic pressure\n\nVon der Leyen said 60 banks will be removed from the Society for Worldwide Interbank Financial Telecommunication (SWIFT) messaging system used for international payments under the new proposals, which are intended to maintain economic pressure on Russia, almost five years into its full-scale invasion of Ukraine.\n\nShe announced the creation of a crypto sanctions task force seeking to curb countries helping Russia to evade sanctions.\n\nThe commission also proposed a ban on visas for Russians who had fought in the war in Ukraine.\n\n“Russian ex-combatants pose a security risk to Europe. They cannot be allowed to wander freely across our countries,” von der Leyen said.\n\n‘Propaganda activities’\n\nThe plan also put forward restrictions on financial flows from Russian sponsors to sports and cultural associations based in the EU. Russian nationals would also no longer be allowed to hold leadership positions on the governing bodies of sports federations.\n\n“Sports events cannot be a forum for propaganda activities,” said von der Leyen.\n\nThe proposed package must be endorsed by all 27 EU member countries before going into force and comes just over a month after the bloc posted the largest listing of sanctions against Russia, targeting about 1,600 Russian officials and companies linked to its arms industry.\n\nThe EU has already slapped sanctions on more than 4,200 people and entities, including Russian President Vladimir Putin and several energy companies.",
