    id: "a641c3d7d8ab3a808769",
    cat: "conflitti",
    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Bukele shares details on deaths of El Salvador’s troops in Haiti",
    summary: "Bukele's military intervention in Haiti comes after four Salvadoran technicians were kidnapped by gangs in October.",
    preview: "Bukele’s military intervention in Haiti comes after four Salvadoran technicians were kidnapped by gangs in October. Salvadoran President Nayib Bukele has identified the three soldiers from his Central American country who were killed while on an anti-gang mission in Haiti. Late on Sunday, Bukele posted on social media that their names were Lieutenant Cristian Daniel Palacios Santos and Corporals Emerson Alexander Zepeda Zelada and Wilber Manuel Serrano Medina. “Tragically, an accidental explosion during that mission claimed their lives,” Bukele said.",
    body: "Bukele’s military intervention in Haiti comes after four Salvadoran technicians were kidnapped by gangs in October.\n\nSalvadoran President Nayib Bukele has identified the three soldiers from his Central American country who were killed while on an anti-gang mission in Haiti.\n\nLate on Sunday, Bukele posted on social media that their names were Lieutenant Cristian Daniel Palacios Santos and Corporals Emerson Alexander Zepeda Zelada and Wilber Manuel Serrano Medina.\n\n“Tragically, an accidental explosion during that mission claimed their lives,” Bukele said.\n\nHe also described instances in which the men had shown “bravery” in the past.\n\nDuring “tests in El Salvador”, Bukele said that Zepeda Zelada and Serrano Medina took quick action to disarm a grenade lodged in a drone.\n\n“It was not possible to steer the drone toward a completely safe area for it to crash,” Bukele said. “They were fully aware of the immense risk they were taking, yet they chose to act to protect others. And they succeeded.”\n\nAccording to Bukele, Palacios Santos was admired for recently taking control during a “critical moment” in an operation in which some contractors allegedly retreated.\n\n“He acted with speed and determination, managing to save several lives, including those of his own colleagues,” Bukele wrote. “We all applaud his bravery.”\n\nHe didn’t identify the contractors.\n\nBukele also used his post to pledge that Salvadoran troops who want to leave Haiti could do so immediately without facing reprisals.\n\nBukele makes a promise to Salvadorans\n\nThe Salvadoran soldiers killed last week were among 2,500 deployed to Haiti last month after four of their own went missing during an October 18 attack in gang-held territory. They have not yet been found.\n\nBukele said that his government would award the families of the three soldiers killed on Friday $200,000 in addition to a monthly income.\n\nHe also stressed that any soldier who wants to leave Haiti immediately may do so “without fear of reprisal and without concern that their return might jeopardise the mission, as many of their comrades have volunteered to relieve anyone who wishes to be relieved”.\n\nSalvadoran troops have come under increased scrutiny as experts and the United Nations note they are operating in a largely unilateral way. Thousands of Haitians recently demonstrated to support Bukele.\n\nThe agreement between Haiti and El Salvador hasn’t been made public.\n\n“There is no information on the scope and limits of its mission, nor on its accountability mechanisms,” according to a report published Monday by NAL Human Rights, a Netherlands-based consulting company focused on Central American issues.\n\nIt noted that, regardless of what that deal stipulates, Salvadoran and international laws require that El Salvador’s armed forces comply with human rights and humanitarian laws.\n\nThe report also underscored that the Central American country and the International Criminal Court have jurisdiction to prosecute Salvadoran military personnel if they are accused of war crimes, genocide or crimes against humanity.\n\nMore than 20,000 people have been killed in gang-related violence across Haiti in the past five years.\n\nUS lawmakers demand answers about the situation in Haiti\n\nThe deaths of the Salvadoran soldiers come as local and international forces fight powerful gangs entrenched in Martissant, a community located in a key corridor that links Haiti’s capital to the country’s western and southern regions.\n\nDozens of suspected gang members have been killed since last week as the battle in Martissant continues, police have said.\n\nIt is the first major operation in Martissant in the more than five years that gangs have controlled it.\n\nAs Haitian police and others continue to battle gangs in Martissant, a UN-backed gang suppression force said it killed several suspected gang members on Sunday as part of ongoing operations in Tabarre and Cite Soleil, located in Port-au-Prince, and in the northern town of Gros Morne.\n\nAs fighting intensifies, two Democratic lawmakers on Monday accused the administration of US President Donald Trump of failing to “name and sanction the political and economic elite fueling Haiti’s insecurity”.\n\nThe concern was outlined in a letter penned by New Hampshire Senator Jeanne Shaheen, the ranking member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, and New York Representative Gregory Meeks, the ranking member of the House Foreign Affairs Committee.\n\n“At a time when the Haitian people are preparing to vote for the first time in over a decade, Congress, the American public and the Haitian people deserve transparency about the nature and scale of collusion between gangs and their political and economic support networks,” they said.\n\nThe letter was addressed to US Secretary of State Marco Rubio, Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth and Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent.\n\nHaiti is scheduled to hold the first round of elections in July 2027. Among the candidates running are some previously sanctioned by the United States and Canada.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "Al Jazeera",
    url: "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/24/bukele-shares-details-on-deaths-of-salvadoran-troops-in-haiti-intervention?traffic_source=rss",
    time: "3 ore fa",
    pub_ts: 1795482360,
