    id: "3239772810e4175d2e8a",
    cat: "conflitti",
    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Attacks on two Russian ships in Libyan waters mark new turn in Ukraine war",
    summary: "Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky claimed seven naval drones carried out two attacks off the coast of Benghazi. The attack on Russian vessels transporting weaponry demonstrates Kyiv's capacity to strike Moscow's interests wherever they might be.",
    preview: "Benghazi, the principal city of eastern Libya, was transformed into an outpost in the war between Russia and Ukraine on Sunday, November 22. The attack on two Russian ships destroyed by Ukrainian naval drones marked an unprecedented extension of the conflict to the southern shore of the Mediterranean. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky claimed responsibility on Monday for the double assault, saying it was carried out by \"seven Ukrainian-made naval drones\" and describing the operation as \"an exemplary Ukrainian operation in response to Russia's war.\"…",
    body: "Benghazi, the principal city of eastern Libya, was transformed into an outpost in the war between Russia and Ukraine on Sunday, November 22. The attack on two Russian ships destroyed by Ukrainian naval drones marked an unprecedented extension of the conflict to the southern shore of the Mediterranean. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky claimed responsibility on Monday for the double assault, saying it was carried out by \"seven Ukrainian-made naval drones\" and describing the operation as \"an exemplary Ukrainian operation in response to Russia's war.\"\n\nThe successful Ukrainian military projection onto the Libyan coast made an even greater impression because attacks against ships carrying Russian weaponry have been so rare. It puts Khalifa Haftar, the de facto leader of Cyrenaica who put his bases at the service of Moscow's African strategy, in a difficult position. The strikes also highlighted the vulnerability of Russia's presence in eastern Libya, where Russian President Vladimir Putin had been forced to move many of his military assets after losing his sanctuary in Syria following the fall of President Bashar al-Assad in December 2024.\n\nYou have 85.31% of this article left to read. The rest is for subscribers only.",
