    title: "AirAsia third-quarter loss narrows, but cash dwindles and liabilities rise",
    summary: "Malaysian budget airline AirAsia has said it is grappling with its most challenging financial period since the pandemic after the Iran war led to a surge in jet fuel prices.",
    preview: "Malaysian budget airline AirAsia has said it is grappling with its most challenging financial period since the pandemic after the Iran war led to a surge in jet fuel prices. Malaysian budget airline AirAsia posted on Monday (Nov 23) a net loss of RM381.3 million (US$92.5 million) for the third quarter, as elevated jet fuel prices weighed on its operations. The loss for the three months ended Sep 30 was narrower than a net loss of RM830.5 million in the second quarter. The loss attributable to equity holders was RM175 million.",
    body: "Malaysian budget airline AirAsia has said it is grappling with its most challenging financial period since the pandemic after the Iran war led to a surge in jet fuel prices.\n\nMalaysian budget airline AirAsia posted on Monday (Nov 23) a net loss of RM381.3 million (US$92.5 million) for the third quarter, as elevated jet fuel prices weighed on its operations.\n\nThe loss for the three months ended Sep 30 was narrower than a net loss of RM830.5 million in the second quarter. The loss attributable to equity holders was RM175 million.\n\nRevenue fell 8.4 per cent to RM4.66 billion from RM5.09 billion in the prior quarter, as AirAsia trimmed capacity by 12 per cent quarter-on-quarter amid fleet rationalisation. About 31 per cent of its 234-strong fleet was out of service at the end of the quarter.\n\nSoutheast Asia's biggest budget carrier has said it is grappling with its most challenging financial period since the pandemic after the Iran war led to a surge in jet fuel prices.\n\nForeign exchange remained a drag. AirAsia booked a net foreign exchange loss of RM141.9 million due to the depreciation of local currencies against the US dollar. No dividend was declared.\n\nAirAsia did not provide year-earlier comparisons due to an accounting treatment arising from its January acquisition of the aviation business of affiliate Capital A.\n\nPREPARING FOR PEAK-SEASON TRAVEL\n\nAirAsia said it was \"cautiously optimistic\" that peak seasonal demand and fleet modernisation would strengthen its resilience heading into 2027. It said it was adding capacity on busy domestic and international routes to capture year-end demand.\n\nIt also said fourth-quarter fuel costs were still expected to be higher than in the third quarter despite a recent easing in jet fuel prices.\n\nThe airline reported cash and bank balances of RM905.3 million and current liabilities of RM19.2 billion as of Sep 30, compared to RM954 million and RM18.4 billion as of Jun 30.\n\nAirAsia controls about 60 per cent of Malaysia's domestic aviation market and employs about 26,000 people. Its stock has lost about 75 per cent of its value this year.\n\nThe carrier has said it is aiming to raise up to US$1 billion from international debt markets plus RM700 million in local credit facilities, primarily to refinance existing debt.\n\nCo-founder Tony Fernandes said last month that he expected the fundraising to be completed between December and February. The airline on Monday gave no new fundraising details, saying only that it was in \"active engagements\" with local and foreign financial institutions and progressing \"liquidity-enhancing exercises\".",
